From: H713 maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] h713: keep comm-trace markers off k0/k1

The CALL/RETURN comm-trace markers stored their stage code with

	lui  k0, <trace page>
	ori  k1, zero, <stage>
	sw   k1, off(k0)

in ordinary task/HISR context. k0/k1 belong to exception handlers: the
display firmware's interrupt exit (0x8b101c2c/0x8b101c30) loads k0 with the
saved Status and k1 with the EPC immediately before eret. An interrupt
between the lui/ori and the store left k0 holding Status, so the store
faulted. The stock fatal-dump handler then faults on its own misaligned
BadVAddr store (0x8b15bc70) and recurses, and its stack walks down through
physical DRAM writing {0x8baa0000, 0x8b15bd44} every 32 bytes. Linux saw
that as kernel paging faults on 0x8b15bd448baa0000, silent resets and, once,
a corrupted inode-table block on disk.

A bench run with the dump handler made survivable preserved the first
fault: EPC 0x8b1004c8, the `sw k1, 0(k0)` of marker 0xa002. Dense CPU_COMM
interrupts (Linux IRQ-driven reception) hit the window within a few hundred
calls; polling hits it far more rarely.

Rewrite every cave word in the comm-trace table from k0 to t8 and from k1
to t9 (155 words: 52 lui, 51 ori, 52 stores). The hooks replace calls,
returns or plain instructions at points where no path reads t8/t9 before
writing them, as patches/u-boot/tests/t8t9-liveness.py proves for all 61
sites in the pinned image; the existing data hooks already rely on t8/t9
the same way, and the firmware's interrupt frame saves both. Relocation
now recognises only t8 bases/stores and its 61/80 counts are unchanged.
The apply loop refuses any cave word that names k0 or k1.

diff --git a/arch/arm/mach-sunxi/h713_mips.c b/arch/arm/mach-sunxi/h713_mips.c
index 175ee27..a4744f2 100644
